/// Storage-provider config panels (#19 × #22). The #22 provisional
/// ruling, verbatim: "third-party visor-ish things (a storage backend's
/// config panel) are APPS — own sandboxed region, own grants, launched
/// FROM the visor, never rendered AS the visor." So each provider ships a
/// panel component: the visor owns the storage page's frame and mounts the
/// panel in a granted subtree; the panel renders provider-specific fields and
/// returns an opaque config blob which the visor carries to the engine's
/// init-store. Credentials never touch app code OR visor-rendered
/// provider code.
///
/// Protocol: the visor calls `seed` (current config JSON, "" for none),
/// then `run`; pumps `on-event`. COMMIT IS THE VISOR'S: the Save/Cancel
/// affordances live outside the granted region, in the visor's own pixels,
/// and the visor calls `commit` when the user saves. A panel that owned its
/// own Save button would own the user's sense of what saving means —
/// exactly the borrowed-authority hazard #22 is about.
///
/// `outcome` remains in the world for the cancelled/completed protocol,
/// but the visor now drives it: panels return none.
package polyvisor:panel@0.1.0;

/// The fixed credential vocabulary (#22): a panel may REQUEST credential
/// kinds; the visor renders the fields itself, outside the granted region,
/// with the visor's own labels — never a panel-supplied string, so a panel
/// cannot make the visor's pixels ask for "your Dropbox password". Values
/// never enter the panel.
interface credentials {
  enum credential-kind {
    access-key,
    secret-key,
    bearer-token,
    refresh-token,
    /// Provider-console identifiers. Strictly speaking these are PUBLIC
    /// inside a shipped client — an app key is compiled into every copy
    /// of the binary, and an app secret is unusable in a public PKCE
    /// client. But the user pastes them out of a provider console that
    /// labels them secrets, and a teachable rule with exceptions is not
    /// a teachable rule: EVERYTHING copied from a provider console is
    /// typed into the visor's sheet, so the rule stays exception-free.
    /// Panels keep only provider-specific NON-secret config (S3:
    /// endpoint, bucket; Dropbox: root folder).
    app-key,
    app-secret,
  }
}

/// Visor-brokered OAuth (#22 × #7): navigation and redirect handling are
/// VISOR capabilities — a sandboxed panel cannot open popups or follow
/// redirects, and must not see the authorization ceremony at all. The visor
/// runs the provider's PKCE dance outside the sandbox and KEEPS the
/// spoils: the resulting tokens are deposited into the visor's own
/// credential fields (see `credentials`), never handed back across the
/// boundary, so a panel learns only success/failure. (In the framework
/// this is a powerbox flow: the visor's own surface shows WHAT is being
/// authorized and holds the resulting capability; the panel never sees
/// the ceremony or its spoils.)
///
/// `authorize` takes NO parameter. The client identifier is the visor's:
/// it is one of the fields the user fills in the visor's own sheet
/// (`credential-kind.app-key`), so a panel naming the client it wants
/// authorized would be a panel influencing where the visor's ceremony
/// points — the same borrowed-authority hazard the sheet exists to
/// close.
///
/// NOTHING IMPORTS THIS TODAY: the visor now triggers the ceremony itself,
/// from a Connect control it renders among the drawer's fields, because
/// that is where the app key lives. The interface stays in the file as
/// the RECORDED SHAPE for future surfaces that may legitimately need to
/// trigger a ceremony they must not observe — and the Dropbox panel's
/// import was dropped rather than left dangling, since an unused
/// capability is a wrong grant (#21).
interface oauth-broker {
  authorize: async func() -> result<_, string>;
}

/// The S3 panel is PURE — dom/events/shell only, no egress: the
/// #21 capability-profile contrast made visible next to its sibling.
world s3-panel {
  import polyvisor:surface/dom@0.1.0;
  import polyvisor:surface/events@0.1.0;
  import polyvisor:surface/shell@0.1.0;

  use polyvisor:surface/events@0.1.0.{event};
  use credentials.{credential-kind};

  export seed: async func(config: string);
  export run: async func();
  export on-event: async func(ev: event);
  export outcome: async func() -> option<string>;

  /// Declared once after `run`; the visor renders these in its own pixels.
  export credential-needs: func() -> list<credential-kind>;

  /// The destination this panel's credentials are bound to: a URL origin
  /// (scheme://host[:port]) or "" while none is configured. The visor
  /// re-reads this after every event, DISPLAYS it in its own credential
  /// fields, and refuses to release credentials toward anything else —
  /// the binding is the visor's, the panel only reports where its config
  /// points. (#22; the enforcement of the engine's actual egress is #7's
  /// network grant, out of scope here.)
  export destination: func() -> string;

  /// What this component CALLS ITSELF. Self-declared and therefore
  /// unverified: the visor reads it once at mount and renders it ONLY as
  /// foreign-quoted text, clamped, never joined into the visor's own
  /// sentence and never used as a lookup key. The unforgeable identity
  /// is the artifact name the visor fetched (provenance); the name the
  /// USER trusts is the petname the user types in the visor's own pixels.
  /// Three names, strictly separated — a self-declared name that could
  /// address a trust record would be impersonation through the table.
  export nickname: func() -> string;


  /// WHAT THIS COMPONENT ASKS TO WEAR — a pet-icon nomination (#22).
  ///
  /// The visor's trust table gives each component a MARK: one glyph the
  /// USER picks, in the visor's own pixels, from a vocabulary the visor
  /// curates. This export lets a component ASK for a particular glyph,
  /// and it is deliberately the weakest possible form of asking:
  ///
  ///   - read ONCE at mount, alongside `nickname`, and never again;
  ///   - rendered in exactly ONE place, inside the naming ceremony's
  ///     picker, FOREIGN-ATTRIBUTED — the visor says the sentence, the
  ///     glyph is quoted as the component's, the way a nickname is;
  ///   - offered only if it is a member of the visor's curated
  ///     vocabulary AND no other trust record already wears it;
  ///     otherwise it is dropped in silence;
  ///   - never a lookup key (the key is provenance, as always);
  ///   - never spoken in the visor's own voice unless the USER adopts
  ///     it, at which point it is the user's mark, not the component's;
  ///   - and the component NEVER LEARNS THE OUTCOME. There is no
  ///     companion import, no return path, no observable difference
  ///     between "adopted", "refused" and "never opened the ceremony".
  ///     The nomination is write-only, for the same reason the petname
  ///     is unreadable: a component that could tell what the user
  ///     decided about it could act on the user's trust in itself.
  ///
  /// `none` = no preference, which is the honest default; the visor then
  /// offers six glyphs of its own.
  export mark-nomination: func() -> option<string>;
  /// Visor-driven commit: the visor owns the Save/Cancel affordances (they
  /// live outside the granted region), and asks the panel to produce its
  /// configuration when the user commits. `none` = not valid yet; the
  /// panel should render its own reason.
  export commit: async func() -> option<string>;
}

/// The Dropbox panel carries ONE granted capability the S3 panel does
/// not: a fetch import the visor scopes to api.dropboxapi.com (the
/// per-destination network grant, used by "test connection"). Its
/// capability profile is visibly non-pure — the #21 egress-badge story
/// in one page.
///
/// It does NOT import `oauth-broker`. Sign-in is triggered from the visor's
/// credential drawer, where the app key is entered; this panel has no
/// reason to name a ceremony it cannot see, and an unused capability is
/// a wrong grant (#21) — so the import is gone, not merely unused.
world dropbox-panel {
  import polyvisor:surface/dom@0.1.0;
  import polyvisor:surface/events@0.1.0;
  import polyvisor:surface/shell@0.1.0;
  import polyvisor:fetch/fetch@0.1.0;

  use polyvisor:surface/events@0.1.0.{event};
  use credentials.{credential-kind};

  export seed: async func(config: string);
  export run: async func();
  export on-event: async func(ev: event);
  export outcome: async func() -> option<string>;

  /// Declared once after `run`; the visor renders these in its own pixels.
  export credential-needs: func() -> list<credential-kind>;

  /// The destination this panel's credentials are bound to: a URL origin
  /// (scheme://host[:port]) or "" while none is configured. The visor
  /// re-reads this after every event, DISPLAYS it in its own credential
  /// fields, and refuses to release credentials toward anything else —
  /// the binding is the visor's, the panel only reports where its config
  /// points. (#22; the enforcement of the engine's actual egress is #7's
  /// network grant, out of scope here.)
  export destination: func() -> string;

  /// What this component CALLS ITSELF — see `s3-panel.nickname`. Read
  /// once at mount, rendered only as foreign-quoted text, never a key.
  export nickname: func() -> string;


  /// WHAT THIS COMPONENT ASKS TO WEAR — a pet-icon nomination (#22).
  ///
  /// The visor's trust table gives each component a MARK: one glyph the
  /// USER picks, in the visor's own pixels, from a vocabulary the visor
  /// curates. This export lets a component ASK for a particular glyph,
  /// and it is deliberately the weakest possible form of asking:
  ///
  ///   - read ONCE at mount, alongside `nickname`, and never again;
  ///   - rendered in exactly ONE place, inside the naming ceremony's
  ///     picker, FOREIGN-ATTRIBUTED — the visor says the sentence, the
  ///     glyph is quoted as the component's, the way a nickname is;
  ///   - offered only if it is a member of the visor's curated
  ///     vocabulary AND no other trust record already wears it;
  ///     otherwise it is dropped in silence;
  ///   - never a lookup key (the key is provenance, as always);
  ///   - never spoken in the visor's own voice unless the USER adopts
  ///     it, at which point it is the user's mark, not the component's;
  ///   - and the component NEVER LEARNS THE OUTCOME. There is no
  ///     companion import, no return path, no observable difference
  ///     between "adopted", "refused" and "never opened the ceremony".
  ///     The nomination is write-only, for the same reason the petname
  ///     is unreadable: a component that could tell what the user
  ///     decided about it could act on the user's trust in itself.
  ///
  /// `none` = no preference, which is the honest default; the visor then
  /// offers six glyphs of its own.
  export mark-nomination: func() -> option<string>;
  /// Visor-driven commit: the visor owns the Save/Cancel affordances (they
  /// live outside the granted region), and asks the panel to produce its
  /// configuration when the user commits. `none` = not valid yet; the
  /// panel should render its own reason.
  export commit: async func() -> option<string>;
}
